{
 "summary": "The Lean theorem bounds the torus inverse `inverse d f`, sliced at a parameter p, for a smooth periodic family f. For every mixed derivative word w of length r it gives a pointwise bound at every point Y of the form mixedLossConstant r · C. Here C is a common bound on |f|, on the pure (r+5)-th x-derivative and on the pure (r+5)-th y-derivative of the slice, all over the unit square. This is a version of the estimate in (8.19), but the most important caveat is a derivative-loss mismatch: Lean loses r+5 derivatives where the paper claims m+4. The existence, uniqueness and Fourier-series parts of Lemma 8.6 are not covered by this statement.",
 "glossary": [
  {"decl": "partialX", "line": 4, "plain": "∂_x f(z) computed as the Fréchet derivative applied to (1,0); it is 0 at points where f is not differentiable", "standard_concept": "first partial derivative in x", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "The junk value is irrelevant because f is C^∞ (hf)."},
  {"decl": "xJet", "line": 6, "plain": "∂_x^p f, the p-fold iterate of partialX", "standard_concept": "pure p-th x-derivative", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "derivativeWord", "line": 9, "plain": "Applies the coordinate partials ∂_{j_1}⋯∂_{j_r}, with each Bool selecting x or y; this gives a mixed derivative of order r = |w|", "standard_concept": "mixed partial ∂^α with |α| = r", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "coordinatePartial is not shown in the excerpt."},
  {"decl": "mixedLossConstant", "line": 14, "plain": "C_r = 6‖ω⁻¹‖·‖ω‖^r·3^{r+5}·∑_k weight(k)^{-4}", "standard_concept": "the constant C_m in (8.19)", "matches_standard": "unclear", "note": "If the tsum is not summable it equals 0, which would make the bound trivially strong. weight, omega and Frequency are not shown. The constant does not depend on d or t."}
 ],
 "lean_claims": [
  {"theorem": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 19, "plain": "Let d be a Direction, f a C^∞ periodic family, p a parameter, w a word with |w| = r, and Y a point of the plane. Suppose that on [0,1]^2 all of |f(p,·)|, |∂_x^{r+5} f(p,·)| and |∂_y^{r+5} f(p,·)| (the latter via swap) are ≤ C. Then |∂^w (inverse d f)(p,·)(Y)| ≤ mixedLossConstant(r)·C."}
 ],
 "paper_claims": [
  {"ref": "Lemma 8.6 (existence/uniqueness)", "plain": "Let F be smooth with zero mean. Then Nφ = F has a unique smooth zero-mean solution, given by the Fourier series Σ_{k≠0} F̂(k)/(2πi v_t·k) e^{2πik·y}."},
  {"ref": "Lemma 8.6, eq. (8.19)", "plain": "For every m ≥ 0, ‖N^{-1}F‖_{C^m_y} ≤ C_m ‖F‖_{C^{m+4}_y}."}
 ],
 "coverage": [
  {"paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6, eq. (8.19)", "lean_theorem": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "relation": "different", "why": "Lean needs r+5 derivatives where the paper uses m+4, which is weaker. Lean controls F only through its pure x/y derivatives and sup norm, not the full C^{m+4} norm, which is stronger. The resulting bound is pointwise for each word of length m; the C^m sup over all orders follows by taking maxima."},
  {"paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6 (existence/uniqueness)", "lean_theorem": null, "relation": "not_formalized", "why": "Neither the solution property, nor uniqueness, nor the Fourier formula appears in this statement."}
 ],
 "flags": [
  {"id": "F1", "category": "goal-substitution", "severity": "medium", "decl": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 23, "paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6, eq. (8.19)", "explanation": "The hypotheses use xJet (w.length + 5), so Lean loses 5 derivatives where the paper claims a loss of 4 (C^{m+4}). The Lean bound therefore needs more regularity and is weaker than (8.19) as stated. The paper's proof sketch says four extra derivatives leave a summable (1+|k|)^{-3} series, which is consistent with a loss of 4.", "polarity": "lean weaker", "probe_idea": "State the same theorem with w.length + 4 in place of w.length + 5 and try to prove it."},
  {"id": "F2", "category": "coverage", "severity": "medium", "decl": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 19, "paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6", "explanation": "Several parts of the lemma are not stated here: existence of a solution to Nφ = F, uniqueness of the zero-mean solution, and the Fourier-series formula. The statement also assumes no zero-mean hypothesis on F, so `inverse` presumably drops the zero mode; its definition is not shown.", "polarity": "lean weaker", "probe_idea": "Prove that N(inverse d f) = f - mean f, and that inverse d f has zero mean."},
  {"id": "F3", "category": "definition-mismatch", "severity": "medium", "decl": "mixedLossConstant", "line": 14, "paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6", "explanation": "The definitions of inverse, Direction, omega, weight and Frequency are not shown. The paper's operator uses v_t from (6.2), with the divisor bound |v_t·k|^{-1} ≤ C(1+|k|). It is unclear whether `inverse d` with a fixed omega captures v_t·∂_y. The constant does not depend on d.", "polarity": "unclear", "probe_idea": "Unfold `inverse` and check that its Fourier multiplier is 1/(2πi v_t·k), with v_t matching (6.2)."},
  {"id": "F4", "category": "junk-value", "severity": "low", "decl": "mixedLossConstant", "line": 16, "paper_ref": null, "explanation": "If (weight k ^ 4)⁻¹ is not summable, the tsum is 0, the constant is 0, and the claimed bound becomes very strong (likely false). Since the theorem is proved, this is harmless for faithfulness. It is still worth confirming that the constant is positive.", "polarity": "lean stronger", "probe_idea": "Prove Summable (fun k : Frequency => (weight k ^ 4)⁻¹) and 0 < mixedLossConstant r."},
  {"id": "F5", "category": "other", "severity": "low", "decl": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 21, "paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6, eq. (8.19)", "explanation": "The hypotheses bound only |f| and the pure ∂_x^{r+5} and ∂_y^{r+5} derivatives on the unit square, not all mixed derivatives. Periodicity makes the unit square sufficient. Requiring only pure derivatives is a strengthening relative to a full C^{m+4} norm, apart from the derivative count in F1.", "polarity": "lean stronger", "probe_idea": null}
 ],
